<title>FRANCE: French tripe factory fined for false beef labels.</title>
<headline>French tripe factory fined for false beef labels.</headline>
<dateline>STRASBOURG, France 1997-03-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>A court in the eastern French city of Strasbourg on Thursday slapped a 30,000 franc ($5,200) fine on a tripe factory for misleading customers about the origin of beef giblets it sold last year.</p>
<p>Marcel Hauck, manager of the Triperies et Boyauderies Reunies d'Alsace, was also ordered to pay 7,000 francs ($1,200) to a meat producers' trade organisation for having sold beef giblets from Germany, Denmark and the Netherlands under a French label in March and April of last year.</p>
<p>Fears over the origin of beef arose early last year amid evidence that people could contract a fatal brain disease by eating beef from animals infected with Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (BSE), known as madcow disease</p>
<p>The European Union imposed a worldwide ban in March 1996 on beef from Britain, the country that has by far the most cases of BSE.</p>
</text>
